WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Before Vance Air Force Base,
there was a family farm.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.600
In 1941, Ernest Baker sold his land
south of Enid

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.810
to make room for a military
flying school.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.419
Then, something extraordinary
happened.

00:00:12.419 --> 00:00:15.609
His son, Ralph, learned to fly
on that very ground.

00:00:15.609 --> 00:00:18.050
Ralph went on to serve overseas,

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.730
flying more than seventy
wartime missions.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.400
Back home, Enid residents
collected scrap metal,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.589
bought war bonds, and helped
support the war effort.

00:00:26.589 --> 00:00:30.100
Nearly ten thousand pilots
earned their wings at the local airfield

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.420
before its temporary closure
in 1947.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.979
And in 1949, the installation
was renamed for Leon Vance,

00:00:37.979 --> 00:00:40.250
an Enid-born Medal of Honor recipient

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.729
who never returned
from World War Two.

00:00:42.729 --> 00:00:45.600
This Veterans Day, we remember
that military history wasn't only

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.700
made on distant battlefields.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.070
Part of it happened right here.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.500
Discover Enid's remarkable
wartime history at

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.909
Enid Public Record dot com.

00:00:54.909 --> 00:00:56.719
And to every veteran...

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:58.121
Thank you for your service.
